<div>
<h3>1. Identify needs</h3><p>The journey starts with a department purchase request. Add the required items, quantities, specifications, and target date. Send the request for review. The sourcing officer can approve it, ask for changes, or reject it with a reason.</p>
<h3>2. Create the tender</h3><p>The sourcing officer turns an approved request into a tender, with clear requirements, terms, and a submission deadline. The published tender provides the shared requirements that suppliers use to prepare their offers.</p>
<h3>3. Invite suppliers</h3><p>Eligible, approved suppliers receive invitations. Each supplier reviews the tender and accepts or declines the invitation. Accepted suppliers prepare their offers with item prices, tax, extra costs, delivery terms, and supporting documents, then submit before the deadline.</p>
<h3>4. Evaluate offers</h3><p>The sourcing officer reviews offer details and confirms compliance before sending eligible offers to the committee. The four committee members agree financial and technical weights, then score each eligible offer. Their combined results support the recommendation.</p>
<h3>5. Review recommendations</h3><p>The committee prepares its report. The sourcing officer reviews it and can approve it, request changes, or reject it. The sourcing manager reviews the approved report and nominated offer, then refers the proposal to the CEO and Chiefs for their decision.</p>
<h3>6. Award the tender</h3><p>The CEO and Chiefs review the nominated offer and record their decisions. Approval allows the tender to be awarded. Tiryaq records the outcome, closes the tender, and notifies the relevant parties. Requests, offers, evaluations, and approvals remain connected.</p>
</div>
